    "id": "news-1786882763751-0",
    "title": "Skin Stretch Marks: Causes and Treatment Options Explained",
    "original_title": "Jeexjeexyada Maqaarka: Yaa Ku Dhaca, Ma La Daaweyn Karaa?",
    "slug": "skin-stretch-marks-causes-and-treatment-options-explained",
    "summary": "Stretch marks, known medically as striae, are common skin lines that form when skin expands or contracts rapidly. Though not a health risk, they can cause cosmetic concern, and experts say various treatment options exist to reduce their appearance.",
    "content": "Stretch marks, medically referred to as striae, are a common skin condition that occurs when the skin undergoes rapid stretching or shrinking. This causes the collagen and elastin fibers in the dermis to break, leading to the formation of linear marks on the skin's surface. They typically appear on the abdomen, upper arms, thighs, buttocks, breasts, back, and hips, and they may initially be red, purple, or darker than the surrounding skin, fading to a lighter, silvery tone over time.\n\nThe condition is widely observed across diverse populations, but certain groups are particularly susceptible. Individuals who experience sudden weight gain or loss, such as during rapid growth spurts in adolescence, are prone to developing stretch marks. Pregnancy is another major factor, as the skin expands significantly over a short period. Additionally, hormonal changes and the use of certain medications, including corticosteroids, can increase risk.\n\nWhile stretch marks are not physically harmful and rarely cause medical complications, they can lead to psychological distress for some, affecting body image and self-esteem. This is especially relevant in communities where visible skin changes may be scrutinized. For members of the Somali diaspora, who may face cultural pressures regarding appearance, the cosmetic impact can be a source of personal concern, prompting many to seek ways to minimize their visibility.\n\nTreatment options vary in effectiveness and are primarily aimed at improving skin texture and color. Over-the-counter creams containing ingredients like hyaluronic acid or retinoids may offer modest benefits, while more advanced procedures such as laser therapy, microdermabrasion, and chemical peels are available for those seeking more pronounced results. However, dermatologists note that no treatment can completely eliminate stretch marks, and conservative approaches are often recommended.\n\nUltimately, understanding that stretch marks are a normal and widespread occurrence can help reduce stigma. As research continues, new therapies may offer improved outcomes, but for now, embracing skin diversity is a key aspect of managing the condition. Individuals concerned about their skin are advised to consult a healthcare professional for personalized guidance.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/08/16/jeexjeexyada-maqaarka-yaa-ku-dhaca-ma-la-daaweyn-karaa/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=jeexjeexyada-maqaarka-yaa-ku-dhaca-ma-la-daaweyn-karaa",
    "source": "radiodalsan.com",
